tel 1.0

base phoenicia
import soundness

info   TrueType and OpenType fonts as values, and typed typefaces, faces and provisions for setting text
detail
    _Phoenicia_ reads font files and types the way a document names and uses a font. It has
    two layers which meet in the middle.

    The lower layer is the **font file**. An `Sfnt` is the table container which TrueType and
    OpenType share, read from any source of bytes; a `Truetype` is one whose outlines are
    quadratic (`glyf` and `loca`), an `Opentype` one whose outlines are PostScript (`CFF `),
    and `Sfnt(data)` tells them apart by the tables present. The tables parse lazily, each as
    a value: `cmap` maps a character to its `Glyph`, `hmtx` gives the advance width and side
    bearing, `head` the units per em and the bounding box, `name` the family and PostScript
    names, `OS/2` the weight, width and embedding rights, `fvar` a variable font's axes, and
    `GSUB`/`GPOS` the layout features it implements. `Sfnt#width` measures a text in ems, and
    `Truetype#subset` builds a new font of only the glyphs some text needs.

    The upper layer is the **typographic request**. A `Typeface` is a family by its name, which
    is also its type (`Typeface["Inter"]` is a `Typeface of "Inter"`); a `Face` says how it is
    to be set — a `Weight`, a `Slant`, a `Stretch`, positions on any further `Variation` axes,
    and the `Face.Feature`s to enable or disable — and nothing about where the font comes from.
    A `Typesettable` provision says how a `Medium` (the web, print, or any a renderer defines)
    obtains the typeface and which faces it can then set, as a `Coverage`. A `Font` pairs a
    face with the provision in scope for its typeface in a medium, and is the only form in
    which a renderer accepts a font, so a document cannot name a typeface it does not carry,
    nor ask a provision for a face it cannot set.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    trait Paper extends Medium

    object fonts:
      given inter: (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Medium =
        Typesettable.embedded(Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter.ttf"))

    import fonts.inter

    val inter = Typeface["Inter"]
    val heading: Font in Paper = Font.of[Paper](inter.bold.italic)
    val figures = inter.face.enabling(Face.Feature.TabularNumerals)
    ```

    The request is checked twice. Always at runtime, when the font is made: a weight the
    provision lacks, an italic it has not got, a feature its tables do not implement, or an
    axis value outside the font's range raises `Font.Error`, naming the typeface and the
    problem. And, where the provision was made from a classpath file (`Typesettable.embedded
    (cp"/fonts/Inter.ttf")`, whose path becomes the provision's `Locus`) and the face's request
    is recorded in its type (`inter.bold.italic` has `Weights = 700` and `Slanting =
    "italic"`), the compiler reads the file and refuses the pairing while the code compiles,
    with the same message.

    The library is one source set, `phoenicia.core`, which compiles for the JVM, Scala.js and
    Scala Native and depends on nothing platform-specific; `Sfnt(source)` reads from anything
    `Streamable by Data`. The media and their provisions live with the renderers: cataclysm's
    `Web` holds the CSS generic families and the `Web.linked`, `Web.imported` and `Web.local`
    factories, and facsimile's `Print` holds PDF's standard fourteen fonts. Those givens sit in
    the medium's companion, so they need no import. A project's own provisions are givens it
    declares and imports by name, since `import pkg.*` does not import givens.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella: `Coverage`, `Face`, `Font`, `Glyph`,
    `Medium`, `Opentype`, `Sfnt`, `Slant`, `Stretch`, `Truetype`, `Typeface`, `Typesettable`,
    `Variation` and `Weight`.

refer phoenicia.Sfnt
refer phoenicia.Truetype
refer phoenicia.Typeface
refer phoenicia.Face
refer phoenicia.Font
refer phoenicia.Typesettable
refer phoenicia.Coverage

entry .Sfnt   a font file as a value: the sfnt table container TrueType and OpenType share
  detail
      An `Sfnt` is a font file, held as its `#data` and read lazily table by table. The name is
      the format's own: an _sfnt_ is the container — a header, a directory of tagged tables,
      and the tables — which TrueType and OpenType both use, differing only in how they store
      outlines. Everything the two share is defined here: the character map, the horizontal
      metrics, the header, the naming table, the OS/2 metrics, the PostScript metadata, a
      variable font's axes and the layout features.

      `Sfnt(source)` reads a font from anything `Streamable by Data` — a galilei path, a
      classpath resource, a URL — and `Sfnt(data)` wraps bytes already in hand. Either returns
      an `Opentype` if the directory has a `CFF ` table and a `Truetype` otherwise, so a match
      can take the TrueType-only operations where they apply:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val font: Sfnt = Sfnt(cp"/fonts/text.ttf")

      font match
        case truetype: Truetype => truetype.subset(t"Hello")
        case opentype: Opentype => opentype
      ```

      Construction is total: wrapping bytes never fails, and an unreadable directory makes a
      `Truetype`. Each table parses when it is first asked for, so a file which is not a font,
      or which lacks a table an operation needs, raises `Font.Error` at that read, with
      `Font.Error.Reason.MissingTable` naming the table, `MagicNumber` for a `head` table whose
      check constant is wrong, `UnknownFormat` for a `cmap` subtable format the library does not
      read, and `MissingEncoding` for a font with no usable character map. The accessors `#head`,
      `#cmap`, `#hhea`, `#hmtx`, `#maxp`, `#post`, `#os2` and `#name` all take a
      `Tactic[Font.Error]`; `#fontName`, `#familyName`, `#fvar` and `#features` absorb the
      error and answer `Unset` or `Nil`.

      The questions typography code asks most are answered directly: `#glyph` resolves a
      character through the preferred character map, `#advanceWidth` and `#leftSideBearing`
      give a character's metrics in design units, and `#width` measures a text in ems, a typed
      quantity which multiplies by a point size to give a physical width.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def measure(font: Sfnt): Quantity[Ems[1]] = font.width(t"Hello world")
      def metrics(font: Sfnt): (Int, Int) = (font.advanceWidth('H'), font.leftSideBearing('H'))
      ```

      A font's tables are read through `#tables`, a `Map` from `Sfnt.Table.Tag` to the table's
      `TableOffset`; tags the library does not name are dropped from it. `Sfnt.assemble` is the
      inverse: it serialises a set of tables as a font file, which is how `Truetype#subset`
      writes its result.
  refer phoenicia.Truetype
  refer phoenicia.Opentype
  refer phoenicia.Font
  refer phoenicia.Glyph
  entry .apply   reads a font from bytes or from any streamable source, as a `Truetype` or an `Opentype`
      `Sfnt(data)` wraps a `Data` value; `Sfnt(source)` reads any `Streamable by Data over
      Credit` source whole and wraps the result. Both probe the table directory for a `CFF `
      table — PostScript outlines — and return an `Opentype` if it is present, otherwise a
      `Truetype`. The probe is wrapped in `safely`, so bytes with an unreadable directory
      become a `Truetype` rather than failing: construction is always total, and the fault
      surfaces as a `Font.Error` when a table is first read.
  entry .PlatformId   the platform of a `cmap` or `name` record: Unicode, Macintosh, Windows or custom
      An enum whose ordinals are the OpenType platform identifiers 0 to 3. The library reads
      platform identifiers as plain `Int`s in `CmapTable.GlyphEncoding` and `NameTable.Record`,
      ranking Windows and Unicode records above Macintosh ones; the enum names the values.
  entry .EncodingId   the Unicode encoding identifiers of a `cmap` record
      `Unicode1`, `Unicode1_1`, `IsoIec10646`, `Unicode2Bmp`, `Unicode2Full`,
      `UnicodeVariation` and `UnicodeFull`, whose ordinals are the encoding identifiers 0 to 6
      of the Unicode platform. `CmapTable#best` prefers the full-Unicode encodings (4 and 6)
      to the BMP-only ones.
  entry .NameId   the identifiers of the naming table's strings; each case's ordinal is its name id
      `Copyright`, `Family`, `Subfamily`, `UniqueId`, `FullName`, `Version`,
      `PostScriptName`, `Trademark`, `Manufacturer`, `Designer`, `Description`, `VendorUrl`,
      `DesignerUrl`, `License`, `LicenseUrl`, `Reserved`, `TypographicFamily`,
      `TypographicSubfamily`, `CompatibleFullName`, `SampleText`, `PostScriptCidName`,
      `WwsFamily`, `WwsSubfamily`, `LightBackgroundPalette`, `DarkBackgroundPalette` and
      `VariationsPostScriptNamePrefix`, in the order of the OpenType `name` table's ids 0 to
      25. `NameTable#apply` takes one and gives the best record for it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def describe(font: Sfnt): Optional[Text] = font.name(Sfnt.NameId.FullName)
      ```
  entry .Table   the tags of the tables a font may carry, as `Ttf` (TrueType) and `Otf` (OpenType) enums
    detail
        A table's four-character tag identifies it in the directory. The tags are split into
        the TrueType set (`Sfnt.Table.Ttf`: `cmap`, `head`, `hhea`, `hmtx`, `maxp`, `name`,
        `post`, `glyf`, `loca`, `fvar`, …) and the OpenType set (`Sfnt.Table.Otf`: `CFF `,
        `OS/2`, `GSUB`, `GPOS`, `GDEF`, `STAT`, …), both of which are `Sfnt.Table.Tag`s and the
        keys of `Sfnt#tables`. Each companion is an extractor from the tag's text, so a
        directory entry is classified by matching `Sfnt.Table.Otf(tag)` and `Sfnt.Table.Ttf
        (tag)`; a tag neither names is dropped from the table map.
    entry .Tag   the common type of `Ttf` and `Otf` tags
      entry #text   the tag as it is spelled in the file: `cmap`, `OS/2`, `CFF `, `cvt `
          Four characters, including the trailing space of `CFF ` and `cvt `. A TrueType tag is
          lowercase and an OpenType tag uppercase, with `OS/2` and `CFF ` as the exceptions.
    entry .Ttf   the TrueType tables: `avar`, `cmap`, `cvar`, `cvt `, `fpgm`, `fvar`, `gasp`, `glyf`, …
      detail
          `Avar`, `Cmap`, `Cvar`, `Cvt`, `Fpgm`, `Fvar`, `Gasp`, `Glyf`, `Gvar`, `Hdmx`, `Head`,
          `Hhea`, `Hmtx`, `Kern`, `Loca`, `Maxp`, `Meta`, `Name`, `Post`, `Prep`, `Sbix`, `Vhea`
          and `Vmtx`. The companion extracts a case from its text (`Sfnt.Table.Ttf(t"cmap")`),
          with `cvt ` spelled with its trailing space.
      entry .extract   the `Ttf` tag for a four-character text, or `Unset`
    entry .Otf   the OpenType tables: `BASE`, `CFF `, `CFF2`, `COLR`, `GDEF`, `GPOS`, `GSUB`, `OS/2`, …
      detail
          `Base`, `Cbdt`, `Cblc`, `Cff`, `Cff2`, `Colr`, `Cpal`, `Dsig`, `Ebdt`, `Eblc`, `Ebsc`,
          `Gdef`, `Gpos`, `Gsub`, `Hvar`, `Jstf`, `Ltsh`, `Math`, `Merg`, `Mvar`, `Os2`, `Pclt`,
          `Stat`, `Svg`, `Vdmx`, `Vorg` and `Vvar`. The companion extracts a case from its text
          (`Sfnt.Table.Otf(t"OS/2")`), with `OS/2` and `CFF ` spelled as the file spells them.
      entry .extract   the `Otf` tag for a four-character text, or `Unset`
  entry .assemble   serialises tagged tables as a complete sfnt file, with checksums
      `Sfnt.assemble(version, tables)` takes the four-byte version (the first four bytes of a
      font: `0x00010000` for TrueType) and a list of `(tag, bytes)` pairs, and writes the
      header, a directory sorted by tag with the search fields the format requires, and the
      tables four-byte aligned and zero-padded. Each directory entry carries its table's
      checksum, and if a `head` table is among them its checksum adjustment is set so that the
      whole file sums to the format's constant. The `head` table must be supplied with its
      adjustment zeroed. `Truetype#subset` builds its result this way.
  entry #data   the bytes of the font file
  entry #TableOffset   a directory entry: a table's tag, checksum, offset and length
    detail
        The values of `#tables`. The offset and length locate the table within `#data`; the
        checksum is the file's, which the library records but does not verify.
    entry #id   the table's `Sfnt.Table.Tag`
    entry #checksum   the checksum the directory records, as a `B32`
    entry #offset   the table's offset from the start of the file
    entry #length   the table's length in bytes
  entry #numTables   the number of tables the directory declares
  entry #searchRange   the directory's `searchRange` field: the largest power of two not above the count, times 16
  entry #entrySelector   the directory's `entrySelector` field: the log of `searchRange` over 16
  entry #rangeShift   the directory's `rangeShift` field: the count times 16, less `searchRange`
  entry #tables   every table the library names, by tag, with its offset and length
      A `Map[Sfnt.Table.Tag, TableOffset]` read from the directory. Tags which are neither a
      `Ttf` nor an `Otf` case are dropped, so a font's private tables are not represented.
      Reading the map is total; a malformed directory makes it empty, and every table accessor
      then raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable`.
  entry #glyph   the glyph for a character, through the font's preferred character map
      `cmap.glyph(char)`: the glyph is `Glyph[this.type]`, typed to this font, so a glyph of
      one font cannot be used with another. A character the font does not map yields glyph 0,
      the missing-glyph, rather than an error. Raises `Font.Error` with `MissingEncoding` if
      the font has no usable `cmap` subtable, or `MissingTable` if it has no `cmap`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def ids(font: Sfnt): List[Int] = List('A', 'B', 'C').map(font.glyph(_).id)
      ```
  entry #advanceWidth   a character's advance width in the font's design units
      The horizontal advance of the character's glyph from `hmtx`, in units of `head.unitsPerEm`
      to the em. A glyph beyond the table's metric count repeats the last advance, as the format
      prescribes for a monospaced tail. Raises `Font.Error` if `cmap`, `hhea` or `hmtx` is
      missing.
  entry #width   the width of a text in this font, as a quantity in ems
      The sum of the characters' advance widths, divided by the units per em, as a
      `Quantity[Ems[1]]`. An em-width is a typed quantity, so it cannot be mistaken for a pixel
      or point measure; scaling it by a font size is explicit arithmetic.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def measure(font: Sfnt): Quantity[Ems[1]] = font.width(t"AB")
      ```
  entry #leftSideBearing   a character's left side bearing in the font's design units
      The bearing of the character's glyph from `hmtx`, which continues in its own array for
      glyphs beyond the metric count. Raises `Font.Error` if a needed table is missing.
  entry #head   the `head` table: units per em, bounding box, creation dates and the `loca` format
      Unpacked as a `HeadTable`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent and with
      `MagicNumber` if the table's check constant is not the format's `0x5f0f3cf5`.
  entry #cmap   the `cmap` table: the character-to-glyph mappings
      As a `CmapTable`, whose `best` subtable is chosen by Unicode preference and whose `glyph`
      resolves a character. Raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #hhea   the `hhea` table: ascender, descender, line gap and the metric count
      Unpacked as an `HheaTable`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #hmtx   the `hmtx` table: advance widths and left side bearings by glyph
      As an `HmtxTable` sized by `hhea.numberOfHMetrics`; raises `Font.Error` if either table
      is absent.
  entry #maxp   the `maxp` table: the glyph count
      As a `MaxpTable`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #post   the `post` table: italic angle, underline metrics and whether the font is monospaced
      As a `PostTable`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #os2   the `OS/2` table: weight and width classes, typographic metrics, embedding rights
      As an `Os2Table`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #name   the `name` table: the font's localised names, by `Sfnt.NameId`
      As a `NameTable`; raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if absent.
  entry #fontName   the font's PostScript name, or `Unset`
      `name(Sfnt.NameId.PostScriptName)`, with any `Font.Error` absorbed: the name by which
      PDF and PostScript documents reference a font. `Unset` for a font which does not record
      one or has no `name` table.
  entry #familyName   the font's family name, or `Unset`
      `name(Sfnt.NameId.Family)`, with any `Font.Error` absorbed.
  entry #fvar   a variable font's `fvar` table, or `Unset` for a static font
      The axes along which a variable font's outlines move, each with its range. `Coverage.of`
      reads the weight and width ranges from here when the table is present.
  entry #features   the layout features the font implements, by tag, from `GSUB` and `GPOS`
      The feature lists of the two layout tables, as `Face.Feature`s, deduplicated. A font with
      neither table implements none. These are what `Coverage.of` records, so a face which
      enables a feature the font lacks is refused.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def tags(font: Sfnt): List[Text] = font.features.map(_.tag)
      ```
  entry #FvarTable   the font variations table of a variable font
    detail
        The axes of a variable font — `wght`, `wdth`, `ital`, `slnt`, `opsz`, or a font's own
        — each with the minimum, default and maximum values it admits, as `Axis` records.
        Reached through `Sfnt#fvar`.
    entry #axisCount   the number of axes
    entry #axisSize   the size in bytes of each axis record
    entry #axes   the axes, each with its tag, range and name id
    entry #Axis   one axis: its four-character tag, its minimum, default and maximum, and its `name` id
      entry #tag   the axis tag, such as `wght`
      entry #minimum   the smallest value the axis admits
      entry #default   the axis's default value
      entry #maximum   the largest value the axis admits
      entry #nameId   the `name` table id of the axis's display name
  entry #HeadTable   the font header: versions, check constants, units per em, bounding box, flags
    detail
        The `head` table, unpacked field by field in the format's own fixed-width types (`U16`,
        `S16`, `B16`, `B32`, `S64`), so each field reads as the integer or bit pattern the
        format defines. `unitsPerEm` is the scale which relates design units to ems; `xMin`,
        `yMin`, `xMax` and `yMax` are the bounding box of every glyph; `indexToLocFormat` says
        whether `loca` uses short or long offsets.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        def box(font: Sfnt): (Int, Int, Int, Int) =
          (font.head.xMin.int, font.head.yMin.int, font.head.xMax.int, font.head.yMax.int)
        ```
    entry #majorVersion   the table's major version, 1
    entry #minorVersion   the table's minor version, 0
    entry #fontRevisionHigh   the integer part of the font's revision number
    entry #fontRevisionLow   the fractional part of the font's revision number
    entry #checksumAdjustment   the value which makes the whole file sum to the format's constant
    entry #magicNumber   the check constant `0x5f0f3cf5`; `Sfnt#head` raises `MagicNumber` if it differs
    entry #flags   the header's flag bits
    entry #unitsPerEm   the design units in one em, by which advances are divided to give em widths
    entry #created   the creation date, in seconds since 1904
    entry #modified   the modification date, in seconds since 1904
    entry #xMin   the left edge of the bounding box of all glyphs
    entry #yMin   the bottom edge of the bounding box of all glyphs
    entry #xMax   the right edge of the bounding box of all glyphs
    entry #yMax   the top edge of the bounding box of all glyphs
    entry #macStyle   the Macintosh style bits: bold, italic, underline, …
    entry #lowestRecPpem   the smallest readable size in pixels per em
    entry #fontDirectionHint   the deprecated direction hint
    entry #indexToLocFormat   0 for short `loca` offsets (halved, 16-bit), 1 for long (32-bit)
    entry #glyphDataFormat   the glyph data format, 0
  entry #HheaTable   the horizontal header: ascender, descender, line gap, caret slope and the metric count
    detail
        The `hhea` table, unpacked field by field. `ascender`, `descender` and `lineGap` are
        the vertical metrics a line layout needs, in design units; `numberOfHMetrics` is how
        many full (advance, bearing) pairs `hmtx` holds before its monospaced tail.
    entry #majorVersion   the table's major version
    entry #minorVersion   the table's minor version
    entry #ascender   the typographic ascender, in design units
    entry #descender   the typographic descender, in design units, negative below the baseline
    entry #lineGap   the typographic line gap
    entry #advanceWidthMax   the largest advance width in `hmtx`
    entry #minLeftSideBearing   the smallest left side bearing
    entry #minRightSideBearing   the smallest right side bearing
    entry #xMaxExtent   the largest horizontal extent of any glyph
    entry #caretSlopeRise   the rise of the caret's slope; 1 for an upright caret
    entry #caretSlopeRun   the run of the caret's slope; 0 for an upright caret
    entry #caretOffset   the caret's horizontal offset
    entry #reserved0   a reserved field
    entry #reserved1   a reserved field
    entry #reserved2   a reserved field
    entry #reserved4   a reserved field
    entry #metricDataFormat   the metric data format, 0
    entry #numberOfHMetrics   the number of full metric pairs in `hmtx`
  entry #HmtxTable   the horizontal metrics: an advance width and left side bearing per glyph
    detail
        One `HMetrics` pair per glyph up to the count `hhea` declares, after which the last
        advance repeats — a monospaced tail — and the bearings continue in an array of their
        own. `Sfnt#advanceWidth` and `Sfnt#leftSideBearing` look a character's glyph up here.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #count   the number of full metric pairs, from `hhea`
    entry #metrics   the full metric pairs, read once
    entry #advanceWidth   the advance width of a glyph by id, repeating the last for the tail
    entry #leftSideBearing   the left side bearing of a glyph by id, from the pairs or the bearing tail
    entry #HMetrics   one glyph's advance width and left side bearing
      entry #advanceWidth   the horizontal advance, in design units
      entry #leftSideBearing   the left side bearing, in design units
  entry #MaxpTable   the maximum profile; only the glyph count is read
    detail
        The `maxp` table. `glyphCount` sizes the `loca` table and bounds every glyph id.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #glyphCount   the number of glyphs in the font
  entry #PostTable   PostScript metadata: italic angle, underline position and thickness, fixed pitch
    detail
        The `post` table. The italic angle is a fixed-point number of degrees, negative for a
        rightward lean, and is one of the signs `Coverage.of` reads to decide that a file is
        italic.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        def leaning(font: Sfnt): Boolean = font.post.italicAngle != 0.0
        ```
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #italicAngle   the italic angle in degrees, 0 for upright, decoded from 16.16 fixed point
    entry #underlinePosition   the underline's position relative to the baseline
    entry #underlineThickness   the underline's thickness
    entry #monospaced   whether the font is fixed-pitch
  entry #Os2Table   OS/2 and Windows metrics: weight and width classes, embedding rights, typographic metrics
    detail
        The `OS/2` table, read field by field at the offsets its version defines. The weight
        class (1–1000, the scale `Weight` uses) and width class (1–9, which
        `Stretch.widthClass` names) are what `Coverage.of` records for a static font; the
        selection bits say whether the face is italic; the typographic ascender, descender and
        line gap, the Windows ascent and descent, the x-height and the cap height are what a
        PDF font descriptor needs. Fields which a table's version predates are `Unset`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        def vertical(font: Sfnt): (Int, Int) = (font.os2.typoAscender, font.os2.typoDescender)
        ```
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #version   the table's version, which decides which later fields are present
    entry #weightClass   the weight on the 1–1000 scale; `Weight(weightClass)` is the `Weight`
    entry #widthClass   the width class, 1 to 9; `Stretch.widthClass` names it
    entry #fsType   the embedding-rights bits
    entry #familyClass   the IBM font class and subclass
    entry #selection   the `fsSelection` bits: italic, underscore, negative, outlined, strikeout, bold, …
    entry #typoAscender   the typographic ascender
    entry #typoDescender   the typographic descender
    entry #typoLineGap   the typographic line gap
    entry #winAscent   the Windows ascent: the top of the clipping region
    entry #winDescent   the Windows descent: the bottom of the clipping region
    entry #xHeight   the x-height, from version 2 of the table; `Unset` before
    entry #capHeight   the cap height, from version 2 of the table; `Unset` before
    entry #embeddable   whether the font may be embedded in a document
        Installable embedding is 0, and of the restriction bits only `0x0002` forbids embedding
        outright, so preview-and-print and editable fonts count as embeddable.
    entry #italic   whether the selection bits mark the face as italic or oblique
        Bit 0 of `fsSelection` marks an italic face and bit 9 an oblique one; either leans.
  entry #NameTable   the naming table: localised, per-platform strings such as the family and PostScript names
    detail
        The `name` table's records, each a string for one name id on one platform, encoding and
        language. `apply` chooses the best record for a `Sfnt.NameId`: Windows US English first,
        then other Windows and Unicode records, then legacy Macintosh. Windows and Unicode
        strings decode from UTF-16BE, Macintosh strings from MacRoman with ISO-8859-1 as the
        fallback.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #count   the number of name records
    entry #records   every record, with its platform, encoding, language, name id and extent
    entry #apply   the best string for a name id, or `Unset` if the font records none
        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        def postScriptName(font: Sfnt): Optional[Text] = font.name(Sfnt.NameId.PostScriptName)
        ```
    entry #Record   one name record: platform, encoding, language, name id, and where its string lies
      entry #platformId   the platform: 0 Unicode, 1 Macintosh, 3 Windows
      entry #encodingId   the encoding within the platform
      entry #languageId   the language; `0x409` is Windows US English
      entry #nameId   the name id, matched against `Sfnt.NameId#ordinal`
      entry #length   the string's length in bytes
      entry #start   the string's offset within the file
      entry #decode   the string, decoded by its platform's encoding
  entry #CmapTable   the character-to-glyph mappings, as encoding subtables of several formats
    detail
        The `cmap` table holds one or more `GlyphEncoding` subtables, each for a platform and
        encoding, and each in one of the formats the library reads: 0 (a byte-indexed array),
        4 (segmented ranges over the Basic Multilingual Plane), 6 (a dense run) and 12 (groups
        covering all of Unicode). `best` picks the preferred subtable — full Unicode before the
        BMP, Unicode before Windows symbol and Macintosh — and `glyph` resolves a character
        through it. A subtable in any other format raises `Font.Error` with `UnknownFormat`
        when its `format` is first read.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #version   the table's version
    entry #numTables   the number of encoding subtables
    entry #glyphEncodings   every encoding subtable, with its platform, encoding and offset
    entry #best   the preferred subtable, or `Unset` if there are none
        Ranked by (platform, encoding): Windows full Unicode (3, 10) first, then Unicode full
        (0, 4) and (0, 6), Windows BMP (3, 1), Unicode BMP (0, 3), other Unicode, Windows symbol
        (3, 0) and Macintosh Roman (1, 0) last.
    entry #glyph   the glyph for a character through the best subtable
        Raises `Font.Error` with `MissingEncoding` if there is no subtable, and `UnknownFormat`
        if the best one is in a format the library does not read. An unmapped character is
        glyph 0.
    entry #GlyphEncoding   one encoding subtable: its platform, encoding, offset and parsed format
      detail
          The subtable's `format` is parsed once, on first use, into a `Format` whose `glyph`
          does the lookup; the parse is memoised under a mutex so concurrent reads share it.
      entry #platformId   the subtable's platform
      entry #encodingId   the subtable's encoding
      entry #offset   the subtable's offset within the file
      entry #formatId   the subtable's format number, read eagerly
      entry #format   the parsed subtable, raising `Font.Error` with `UnknownFormat` for a format not read
      entry #Segment   a format-4 segment: a character range with its delta and range offset
        entry #start   the first character of the segment
        entry #end   the last character of the segment
        entry #delta   added to a character to give its glyph, when the range offset is zero
        entry #rangeOffset   nonzero when the segment indirects through the glyph id array
      entry #Format   what every parsed subtable can do: map a character to a glyph of this font
        entry #glyph   the glyph for a character, or glyph 0 if unmapped
      entry #Format0   a byte-indexed array of glyph ids, for the first 256 character codes only
        entry #start   the offset of the array
        entry #glyph   the glyph for a character below 256, else glyph 0
      entry #Format4   segmented ranges over the Basic Multilingual Plane, by delta or indirection
        entry #idRangeOffsetsStart   the offset of the range-offset array, to which indirections are relative
        entry #segments   the segments, in order of their end characters
        entry #glyph   the glyph for a character: found by segment, then by delta or through the glyph id array
      entry #Format6   a dense run of glyph ids for a contiguous range of character codes
        entry #first   the first character code of the run
        entry #count   the number of codes in the run
        entry #start   the offset of the glyph id array
        entry #glyph   the glyph for a character within the run, else glyph 0
      entry #Format12   groups of sequential mappings covering all of Unicode, binary-searched
        entry #groupCount   the number of groups
        entry #start   the offset of the group array
        entry #glyph   the glyph for a character, by binary search over the groups

entry .Truetype   a TrueType font: an sfnt whose outlines are quadratic, in `glyf` and `loca`, which can be subset
  detail
      A `Truetype` is an `Sfnt` with the two outline tables — `loca`, the index of where each
      glyph's data lies, and `glyf`, the data itself — and the operations which need them.
      `Truetype(source)` reads one from any streamable source without probing the format;
      `Sfnt(source)` is the usual way in, since it returns a `Truetype` for a font without a
      `CFF ` table.

      The operation that matters is `#subset`: a new font containing only the outlines some
      characters need, for embedding in a document.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def reduce(font: Truetype): Truetype = font.subset(t"Hello world")
      ```

      Subsetting keeps the original glyph numbering — unused glyphs keep their numbers with
      empty outlines — so the character map, the metrics and every reference between glyphs
      remain valid without being rewritten, and every other table is carried over unchanged.
      Composite glyphs are followed: the retained set is the transitive closure under
      composition (`#glyphClosure`), so an accented character keeps the components it is drawn
      from. The result is a complete file, checksums included, with `head` switched to long
      `loca` offsets.
  refer phoenicia.Sfnt
  refer phoenicia.Opentype
  entry .apply   reads a TrueType font from any streamable source
      `Truetype(source)` reads a `Streamable by Data over Credit` source whole; `Truetype(data)`
      is the case-class constructor over bytes. Neither checks the outline format: `Sfnt(…)`
      does, and is what to use when the file might be OpenType.
  entry #data   the bytes of the font file
  entry #loca   the `loca` table: each glyph's offset within `glyf`
      Sized by `maxp.glyphCount` and read in the format `head.indexToLocFormat` names. Raises
      `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if `loca`, `maxp` or `head` is absent.
  entry #glyf   the `glyf` table: the glyph outlines, indexed through `loca`
      Raises `Font.Error` with `MissingTable` if `glyf` or `loca` is absent.
  entry #subset   a new font of only the glyphs some characters need, numbering preserved
      `subset(chars: Set[Char])` or `subset(text: Text)`. The retained glyphs are those the
      characters map to, glyph 0, and the transitive closure under composite components. Every
      other glyph keeps its number and loses its outline; `glyf` and `loca` are rebuilt (the
      latter always in the long format, with `head` adjusted to say so), and every other table
      is copied byte for byte. The whole file is reassembled with `Sfnt.assemble`, so its
      checksums are valid. Raises `Font.Error` if a table the rebuild needs is missing.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def embeddable(font: Truetype): Truetype = font.subset(Set('H', 'e', 'l', 'o'))
      ```
  entry #glyphClosure   the glyphs needed to render a set of glyphs, following composite components
      The transitive closure of a set of glyph ids under `GlyphRecord#components`: a composite
      glyph is drawn from others, which may themselves be composite. `#subset` uses this to
      decide what to keep. Raises `Font.Error` if `glyf` or `loca` is missing.
  entry #LocaTable   the glyph-location index: for each glyph, where its data starts within `glyf`
    detail
        One offset per glyph plus one more, so a glyph's extent is the difference between its
        offset and the next. In the short format the offsets are stored halved in sixteen bits;
        in the long format they are stored as they are.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #glyphCount   the number of glyphs, from `maxp`
    entry #longFormat   whether offsets are 32-bit (`true`) or halved 16-bit (`false`)
    entry #offsets   the offsets, unhalved, read once
  entry #GlyfTable   the glyph outlines, each reached by id as a `GlyphRecord`
    detail
        `glyf(id)` locates a glyph's data through `loca`. The record exposes the raw bytes, the
        contour count, whether the glyph is composite and, if it is, the components it is
        drawn from; outlines themselves are not decoded beyond this.
    entry #offset   the table's offset within the file
    entry #loca   the `loca` table the outlines are indexed through
    entry #apply   the record for a glyph id
    entry #GlyphRecord   one glyph's raw data: its extent, contour count and composite components
      detail
          A glyph with no outline — a space, or a glyph discarded by `#subset` — has zero
          extent; a composite glyph has a negative contour count and lists the glyphs it is
          assembled from.
      entry #start   the glyph data's offset within the file
      entry #length   the glyph data's length; 0 for a glyph without an outline
      entry #empty   whether the glyph has no outline
      entry #bytes   the glyph's raw data
      entry #contourCount   the number of contours, or a negative value for a composite glyph
      entry #composite   whether the glyph is assembled from other glyphs
      entry #components   the ids of the glyphs a composite is drawn from; `Nil` for a simple glyph
          Read once, walking the component records by their flags: word or byte arguments, and a
          single scale, separate x and y scales, or a 2×2 transformation.

entry .Opentype   an OpenType font: an sfnt whose outlines are PostScript, in a `CFF ` table
  detail
      An `Sfnt` whose glyph outlines are stored in `CFF `. Every shared table works —
      character mapping, metrics, names, `OS/2`, `fvar`, layout features — but the outline
      data is not yet parsed, so there is no equivalent of `Truetype#glyf` and no subsetting.
      `Sfnt(source)` returns an `Opentype` when it finds the `CFF ` table; `Opentype(source)`
      reads one without checking.
  refer phoenicia.Sfnt
  refer phoenicia.Truetype
  entry .apply   reads an OpenType font from any streamable source
  entry #data   the bytes of the font file

entry .Glyph   a glyph of one particular font, by its id
  detail
      `Glyph[font.type]` is an opaque wrapper over a glyph id, typed to the font it was
      resolved in, so a glyph of one font cannot be used to index another. `Sfnt#glyph(char)`
      is the way to get one; `glyph.id`, an extension declared in `internal`, reads the id
      back (0 is the missing-glyph). `Glyph(font, id)` wraps an id already known. The type is
      `phoenicia.internal.Glyph`, exported here.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      def glyphId(font: Sfnt): Int = font.glyph('A').id
      ```

      Its companion's members are declared in `internal.Glyph`, from which the type is
      exported, and documented there.
  refer phoenicia.Sfnt

entry .Typeface   a font family by its name, which is also its type: `Typeface["Inter"]` is a `Typeface of "Inter"`
  detail
      A `Typeface` names a family — the name a document writes where it refers to the
      typeface, a CSS `font-family` or a PDF `/BaseFont` — and carries that name as its
      `Topic`, so `Typeface["Inter"]` has the type `Typeface of "Inter"` and a provision for
      one typeface is distinguishable from a provision for another as the code compiles. It
      says nothing about where the font comes from: that is a `Typesettable` provision.

      A typeface's faces are reached fluently. `#face` (or `#regular`) is its regular upright
      face, and `#bold`, `#light`, `#italic` and the rest choose from there; every one is a
      `Face of "Inter"`, which carries its request in its type for the compile-time check.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val inter = Typeface["Inter"]
      val heading = inter.bold.italic
      val figures = inter.face.enabling(Face.Feature.TabularNumerals)
      val display = inter.face.varying(Variation.Axis.OpticalSize, 24.0)
      ```

      The CSS generic families — `.SansSerif`, `.Serif`, `.Monospace`, `.Cursive`,
      `.Fantasy` and `.SystemUi` — are typefaces too; a browser sets each from whatever it
      has, so cataclysm's `Web` companion provides them without anything being declared.
      Two typefaces are equal when their names are, and a typeface shows as its name.
  refer phoenicia.Face
  refer phoenicia.Typesettable
  refer phoenicia.Font
  entry .apply   `Typeface["Inter"]`: the typeface of a family, from its name as a literal type
      An inline method whose type argument is the family name, as a `Label`: the name is read
      from the type with `constValue`, so it must be a literal. The result is `Typeface of
      family`, with the name as both value and type.
  entry .SansSerif   the CSS generic `sans-serif` family, a `Typeface of "sans-serif"`
  entry .Serif   the CSS generic `serif` family, a `Typeface of "serif"`
  entry .Monospace   the CSS generic `monospace` family, a `Typeface of "monospace"`
  entry .Cursive   the CSS generic `cursive` family, a `Typeface of "cursive"`
  entry .Fantasy   the CSS generic `fantasy` family, a `Typeface of "fantasy"`
  entry .SystemUi   the CSS generic `system-ui` family, a `Typeface of "system-ui"`
  entry .showable   shows a typeface as its family name
  entry .inspectable   inspects a typeface as `Typeface["Inter"]`
  entry #name   the family name, as text
  entry #Topic   the family name as a literal type: `"Inter"` for `Typeface["Inter"]`
  entry #face   the typeface's regular, upright face, from which the others are chosen
      A `Face of Topic` at `Weight.Regular`, `Slant.Upright` and `Stretch.Normal`, with no
      variations and no features, whose type records `Weights = 400`, `Slanting = "upright"`
      and `Enabled = Nothing`. `#regular` is the same face.
  entry #regular   the regular upright face; the same as `#face`
  entry #light   the light (300) upright face
  entry #medium   the medium (500) upright face
  entry #semibold   the semibold (600) upright face
  entry #bold   the bold (700) upright face
  entry #black   the black (900) upright face
  entry #italic   the regular italic face

entry .Face   how a typeface is to be set: weight, slant, stretch, variation axes and layout features
  detail
      A `Face` describes a request: a `#typeface` at a `#weight`, a `#slant` and a
      `#stretch`, with `#variations` on any further axes of a variable font and the
      `#features` to enable or disable. It says nothing about where the font comes from;
      pairing it with a `Typesettable` provision makes a `Font`. The typeface's family is the
      face's `Topic`, so `Typeface["Inter"].bold` is a `Face of "Inter"`.

      Faces are built fluently from a typeface's `face`, each method returning a new face
      with one part changed and the type updated to match:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The regular face
        code
            val face = Typeface["Inter"].face
            // Face.Shape["Inter", 400, "upright", Nothing]
      frame
        label Bold
        code
            val face = Typeface["Inter"].face.bold
            // Face.Shape["Inter", 700, "upright", Nothing]
      frame
        label Bold italic
        code
            val face = Typeface["Inter"].face.bold.italic
            // Face.Shape["Inter", 700, "italic", Nothing]
      frame
        label With oldstyle numerals
        code
            val face = Typeface["Inter"].face.bold.italic.enabling(Face.Feature.OldstyleNumerals)
            // Face.Shape["Inter", 700, "italic", "onum"]
      frame
        label A weight decided at runtime
        code
            def weight: Weight = Weight(650)
            val face = Typeface["Inter"].face.weighing(weight).italic.enabling(Face.Feature.OldstyleNumerals)
            // Face.Shape["Inter", Face.Runtime, "italic", "onum"]
      ##
      ```

      Three type members record what the code asked for, where it is known as the code
      compiles: `#Weights` is the weight as a literal (`700`), `#Slanting` is `"upright"` or
      `"italic"`, and `#Enabled` is the union of the enabled features' tags (`"onum" | "tnum"`,
      or `Nothing` for none). A part decided at runtime — `#weighing` with a computed weight,
      `#slanted`, `#featuring` with a feature by its tag — is recorded as `Face.Runtime`. When
      a `Font` is made from a provision whose file is on the classpath, the recorded request
      is checked against the file as the code compiles; the rest is checked when the font is
      made. A face annotated as plain `Face of "Inter"` loses the record, and is checked at
      runtime only.

      `Face(typeface, weight, slant, …)` builds a face from runtime values, with every type
      member `Runtime`. A face shows as `Inter 700 italic`.
  refer phoenicia.Typeface
  refer phoenicia.Font
  refer phoenicia.Weight
  refer phoenicia.Slant
  refer phoenicia.Stretch
  refer phoenicia.Variation
  entry .Runtime   the marker type for a part of a request decided at runtime
      A sealed trait used only as a type: `Weights = Face.Runtime` says the weight was computed
      rather than chosen by name, so the compile-time check leaves it to the runtime check.
  entry .Shape   `Face.Shape[family, weights, slanting, enabled]`: a face with its whole request in its type
      The refinement `Face { type Topic = family; type Weights = weights; type Slanting =
      slanting; type Enabled = enabled }`, which every fluent method returns.
  entry .apply   a face from runtime values: `Face(typeface, weight, slant, stretch, variations, features)`
      Every parameter after the typeface has a default — regular, upright, normal, none,
      none — and the result is `Face.Shape[family, Runtime, Runtime, Runtime]`, so a font made
      from it is checked at runtime only.
  entry .showable   shows a face as its typeface, weight and slant: `Inter 700 italic`
      The slant is omitted when upright: `Inter 400`.
  entry .inspectable   inspects a face as `Face("Inter", Weight(700), Slant.Italic)`
  entry .Feature   an OpenType layout feature by its four-character tag, which is also its type
    detail
        `Face.Feature.OldstyleNumerals` is a `Feature of "onum"`: the tag is the feature's
        `Topic`, so a face which enables it records `"onum"` in its `Enabled` type. The common
        registered features have names; stylistic sets and character variants are numbered
        (`.stylisticSet(3)` is `ss03`); and `Face.Feature(t"…")` admits any other tag, known
        only at runtime as a `Feature of Runtime`.

        A feature becomes a `Setting` by `#enabled`, `#disabled` or `#set(value)`, and a face
        takes settings through `Face#enabling`, `Face#disabling` and `Face#featuring`. Two
        features are equal when their tags are; a feature shows as its tag.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val figures = Typeface["Inter"].face
          . enabling(Face.Feature.TabularNumerals)
          . disabling(Face.Feature.Ligatures)
          . featuring(Face.Feature.stylisticSet(3).enabled)
        ```
    entry .apply   `Face.Feature(tag)`: a feature by any tag, known at runtime only
        The result is a `Feature of Runtime`, so enabling it adds `Face.Runtime` to a face's
        `Enabled` type rather than the tag, and the compile-time check skips it.
    entry .Ligatures   standard ligatures, `liga`
    entry .DiscretionaryLigatures   discretionary ligatures, `dlig`
    entry .HistoricalLigatures   historical ligatures, `hlig`
    entry .ContextualAlternates   contextual alternates, `calt`
    entry .Kerning   kerning, `kern`
    entry .OldstyleNumerals   oldstyle figures, `onum`
    entry .LiningNumerals   lining figures, `lnum`
    entry .TabularNumerals   tabular figures, `tnum`
    entry .ProportionalNumerals   proportional figures, `pnum`
    entry .SlashedZero   a slashed zero, `zero`
    entry .Fractions   fractions, `frac`
    entry .Ordinals   ordinal indicators, `ordn`
    entry .SmallCapitals   small capitals from lowercase, `smcp`
    entry .CapitalSmallCapitals   small capitals from capitals, `c2sc`
    entry .Swashes   swash forms, `swsh`
    entry .Superscript   superscript forms, `sups`
    entry .Subscript   subscript forms, `subs`
    entry .stylisticSet   stylistic set _n_, `ss01` to `ss20`, as a runtime feature
        The number is zero-padded to two digits. The result is a `Feature of Runtime`, since
        the tag is made at runtime.
    entry .characterVariant   character variant _n_, `cv01` to `cv99`, as a runtime feature
    entry .Setting   a feature with the value it is set to: 0 disables, 1 enables, higher selects an alternate
      detail
          What a face holds in its `features` list. Most features are on or off; character
          variants and some others select among alternates by higher values.
      entry #feature   the feature
      entry #value   the value: 0 off, 1 on, or an alternate's number
    entry .showable   shows a feature as its tag
    entry .inspectable   inspects a feature as `Face.Feature("onum")`
    entry #tag   the four-character tag
    entry #enabled   the setting which turns the feature on, value 1
    entry #disabled   the setting which turns the feature off, value 0
    entry #set   the setting with a chosen value, for features which select among alternates
  entry #typeface   the typeface the face is of
  entry #weight   the weight, on the 1–1000 scale
  entry #slant   upright, italic, or oblique at an angle
  entry #stretch   the width as a percentage of normal
  entry #variations   positions on further axes of a variable font
  entry #features   the layout feature settings, in the order they were added
  entry #Topic   the family name as a literal type
  entry #Weights   the requested weight as a literal type (`700`), or `Face.Runtime`
  entry #Slanting   the requested slant as `"upright"` or `"italic"`, or `Face.Runtime`
  entry #Enabled   the tags of the enabled features as a union type, `Nothing` for none, or with `Face.Runtime`
  entry #weighing   the face at any weight, decided at runtime
      `Weights` becomes `Face.Runtime`, so the weight is checked only when the font is made.
      The named weights (`#bold`, `#light`, …) record their literal instead.
  entry #thin   the face at weight 100
  entry #light   the face at weight 300
  entry #regular   the face at weight 400
  entry #medium   the face at weight 500
  entry #semibold   the face at weight 600
  entry #bold   the face at weight 700
  entry #black   the face at weight 900
  entry #slanted   the face at any slant, decided at runtime
      `Slanting` becomes `Face.Runtime`. `#italic`, `#upright` and `#oblique` record the slant
      instead.
  entry #italic   the italic face; `Slanting` becomes `"italic"`
  entry #upright   the upright face; `Slanting` becomes `"upright"`
  entry #oblique   the face slanted at an angle in degrees, leaning right when positive
      An oblique is a slanted upright rather than a separately drawn italic, but it is
      recorded as `"italic"` for the coverage check, since a provision which offers an italic
      face is what sets it.
  entry #stretched   the face at any stretch
      The stretch is not recorded in the type, so it is checked only when the font is made.
  entry #condensed   the face at 75% of normal width
  entry #expanded   the face at 125% of normal width
  entry #varying   the face with a position on a further axis of a variable font
      `varying(Variation.Axis.OpticalSize, 24.0)` adds a `Variation`. The registered weight,
      width, italic and slant axes are set through the weight, stretch and slant instead, so a
      static and a variable font are asked for alike; `varying` is for optical size and a
      font's own axes. An axis the font lacks, or a value outside its range, is refused when
      the font is made.
  entry #featuring   the face with a feature setting made at runtime
      Adds a `Face.Feature.Setting`. The setting may enable anything, so `Enabled` gains
      `Face.Runtime`, and the compile-time check skips what it cannot see.
  entry #enabling   the face with a feature enabled, its tag recorded in the type
      `enabling(Face.Feature.OldstyleNumerals)` adds `"onum"` to `Enabled`, so a provision
      whose file lacks the feature refuses the font as the code compiles.
  entry #disabling   the face with a feature disabled
      Disabling a feature the font lacks changes nothing, so it adds no requirement and the
      type is unchanged.

entry .Weight   a weight on the 1–1000 scale CSS and OpenType share, with the usual names
  detail
      An opaque `Int` (`phoenicia.internal.Weight`, exported here). `Weight(value)` brings a
      value outside the scale to its nearer end rather than rejecting it. The nine named
      weights are `.Thin` (100), `.ExtraLight` (200), `.Light` (300), `.Regular` (400),
      `.Medium` (500), `.Semibold` (600), `.Bold` (700), `.ExtraBold` (800) and `.Black`
      (900). A `.Range` is an inclusive span, as a variable font's `wght` axis or a
      `@font-face` declaration offers, made with `lower to upper` or `weight.range`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val offered: Weight.Range = Weight.Light to Weight.Bold
      offered.contains(Weight(650))
      ```

      Its companion's members are declared in `internal.Weight`, from which the type is
      exported, and documented there.
  refer phoenicia.Face
  refer phoenicia.Coverage

entry .Slant   whether a face leans: upright, a true italic, or an oblique at an angle
  detail
      `Upright` and `Italic` are the two drawn faces; `Oblique(angle)` is a slanted upright at
      an angle in degrees, leaning right when positive. For coverage, an oblique counts as
      italic. A slant shows as `upright`, `italic` or `oblique 11.5°`.
  entry .showable   shows a slant as `upright`, `italic` or `oblique 11.5°`
  entry .inspectable   inspects a slant as `Slant.Upright`, `Slant.Italic` or `Slant.Oblique(11.5)`
  entry #upright   whether the slant is `Upright`

entry .Stretch   a face's width as a percentage of its normal width, from 50% to 200%
  detail
      An opaque `Double` (`phoenicia.internal.Stretch`, exported here), as CSS `font-stretch`
      measures it. `Stretch(percentage)` clamps to 50–200. The nine named widths from
      `.UltraCondensed` (50%) through `.Normal` (100%) to `.UltraExpanded` (200%) are the
      ones the OS/2 table's `usWidthClass` numbers 1 to 9, which `.widthClass` maps. A
      `.Range` is an inclusive span, as a variable font's `wdth` axis offers.

      Its companion's members are declared in `internal.Stretch`, from which the type is
      exported, and documented there.
  refer phoenicia.Face

entry .Variation   a position on one axis of a variable font
  detail
      `Variation(axis, value)` is what `Face#varying` adds to a face. The registered weight,
      width, italic and slant axes are set through a face's `Weight`, `Stretch` and `Slant`,
      so a `Variation` is for optical size and a font's own axes. A provision checks each
      variation against the font's `fvar` ranges: an axis the font lacks is refused with
      `Font.Error.Reason.UnknownAxis`, a value outside the range with `AxisOutOfRange`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val display = Typeface["Inter"].face.varying(Variation.Axis.OpticalSize, 24.0)
      ```
  refer phoenicia.Face
  refer phoenicia.Coverage
  entry .Axis   a variation axis by its four-character tag
    detail
        An opaque `Text`. The five registered axes have names — `.Weight` (`wght`), `.Width`
        (`wdth`), `.Italic` (`ital`), `.Slant` (`slnt`) and `.OpticalSize` (`opsz`) — and a
        font's own axes are made from their tags with `Variation.Axis(tag)`.
    entry .apply   an axis from its tag
    entry .Weight   the `wght` axis
    entry .Width   the `wdth` axis
    entry .Italic   the `ital` axis
    entry .Slant   the `slnt` axis
    entry .OpticalSize   the `opsz` axis
    entry .showable   shows an axis as its tag
    entry .inspectable   inspects an axis as `Variation.Axis("opsz")`
    entry .tag   the four-character tag
  entry .Range   the values a font admits on one axis, from its `fvar` table
    entry #axis   the axis
    entry #lower   the smallest admitted value
    entry #upper   the largest admitted value
    entry #contains   whether a value lies within the range
  entry #axis   the axis
  entry #value   the position on it

entry .Medium   a medium in which text is set: the web, print, or any a renderer defines
  detail
      An open trait. A `Typesettable` provision names the medium it serves as its `Form`,
      and a `Font in Web` is a font for a browser, a `Font in Print` one for a PDF. A
      provision `in Medium` itself — an embedded font file — serves every medium, since a
      file can be carried anywhere, which is why a requester asks for a provision `in (? >:
      medium)` rather than `in medium`.

      Each renderer declares its own medium as a trait with a companion, and the companion is
      where the provisions that medium can always honour live, in implicit scope: cataclysm's
      `object Web` holds the CSS generic families, facsimile's `object Print` holds PDF's
      standard fourteen fonts. A test or a renderer of its own declares one in a line:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      trait Paper extends Medium
      ```
  refer phoenicia.Typesettable
  refer phoenicia.Font

entry .Typesettable   the provision of a typeface in a medium: where the font comes from, and which faces it sets
  detail
      A `Typeface of "Inter" is Typesettable in Web` says how the web obtains Inter — as its
      `#sources`: the font file itself, a URL, a font the reader has installed, a stylesheet,
      or nothing for a generic family — and which faces it can then set, as its `#coverage`.
      The medium served is its `Form`, a type member inherited from `Formal`: `Medium` for
      every medium, or one medium such as `Web`.
      It is declared once, as a given, and a `Font` can be made only where one is in scope
      for the typeface in the medium: that is what guarantees a document carries what its
      medium needs for every typeface it names.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      object fonts:
        val interFile: Sfnt = Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter-Variable.ttf")

        given inter: (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Medium =
          Typesettable.embedded(interFile)
      ```

      `.embedded` makes a provision from font files, which every medium can carry, so it is
      `in Medium`; its coverage is read from the files, and several files — regular, bold,
      italic — make one provision whose coverage is their union. Provisions belonging to one
      medium are declared through that medium: `Web.linked` for a file at a URL,
      `Web.imported` for a stylesheet, `Web.local` for an installed font, each with the
      coverage it is declared to have, since there is no file to read; `Print`'s standard
      fourteen need no declaration, nor do the generic families on the web.

      A requester asks for a provision `in (? >: medium)`: an instance `in Medium` serves
      every medium, while one `in Web` serves only the web, and `Font.of[Print](face)` with
      only a `Web` provision in scope is a compile error. The `@missingContext` message says
      so, and names the two ways to declare one.

      A provision is `Locative`: made from a classpath resource (`Typesettable.embedded
      (cp"/fonts/Inter.ttf")`) it keeps the path as its `Locus`, and a `Font` made from it is
      checked against the file as the code compiles. The whole type is then parenthesised in
      a named given, since an infix chain with a literal type is not admitted in the given's
      type otherwise:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      given inter: ((Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Medium at "/fonts/Inter.ttf") =
        Typesettable.embedded(cp"/fonts/Inter.ttf")
      ```

      Provisions are pure data and live where the medium lives: in the companion of `Web` or
      `Print` for the ones a medium always has, and in an object a project imports by name for
      its own — `import fonts.inter`, since `import fonts.*` does not import givens.
  refer phoenicia.Typeface
  refer phoenicia.Font
  refer phoenicia.Coverage
  refer phoenicia.Medium
  entry .Source   how a medium obtains a typeface: embedded, linked, local, imported, or generic
    detail
        `Embedded(sfnt)` carries the font file itself inside the document; `Linked(url,
        format)` names a file to fetch, with its `Format` if known; `Local(name)` names a font
        the reader already has installed; `Imported(url)` names a stylesheet which declares the
        typeface; and `Generic` is a family every medium can set. A document collects the
        sources of its fonts and emits whatever its medium needs — `@font-face` rules, an
        embedded program.
    entry .Format   the format of a linked font file: `Ttf`, `Otf`, `Woff` or `Woff2`
        What a `@font-face` `src` declares with `format(…)`, so a browser can skip a file it
        cannot read.
  entry .Embedding   the provision `.embedded` returns: files carried inside the document
    detail
        A named class, so the provision has a stable type. It is `in Medium`, its sources are
        one `Source.Embedded` per file, and its coverage is `Coverage.combine` over the files'
        `Coverage.of`.
    entry #Self   the typeface provided
    entry #Form   `Medium`: an embedded file serves every medium
    entry #sources   one `Typesettable.Source.Embedded` per font file
    entry #coverage   the union of the files' coverage, read from their tables
  entry .embedded   a provision from font files, `in Medium`, with coverage read from the files
      Two overloads. `Typesettable.embedded[family](sfnts*)` takes any number of `Sfnt`s
      already in hand and makes a `Typeface of family is Typesettable in Medium`; the family is
      given explicitly or inferred from the given's declared type. `Typesettable.embedded
      (source)` takes one `Locative` streamable source — a `cp"…"` resource — reads it, and
      keeps its path as the provision's `Locus`, so a `Font` made from the provision is checked
      as the code compiles against the file at that path on the compiler's classpath. Where the
      file is not on the compiler's classpath, the check is left to runtime.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      given inter: (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Medium =
        Typesettable.embedded(Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter-Regular.ttf"), Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter-Bold.ttf"))
      ```
  entry #Self   the typeface provided, a `Typeface of family`
  entry #sources   how the medium obtains the typeface, one `Typesettable.Source` per part
  entry #coverage   the faces the provision can set

entry .Coverage   which faces a provision can set: weights, slants, widths, axes and features
  detail
      A `Coverage` is either `Unknown` — nothing is known of the faces a source offers, so
      every face is admitted — or `Entries(entries)`, a list of `Coverage.Entry`s, each one
      face a provision offers or the range of faces a variable font offers. A `Font` is made
      only for a face the coverage admits; `#complaint` gives the `Font.Error.Reason` why it
      is not, or `Unset` if it is.

      Coverage is matched as a browser matches a `@font-face` declaration: the entries which
      span the weight are found first, then of those the ones with the slant, then the
      stretch, and the face's variations and features are checked against what remains. The
      first failing step names the refusal: `UncoveredWeight`, `UncoveredSlant`,
      `UncoveredStretch`, `UnknownAxis`, `AxisOutOfRange` or `MissingFeature`.

      `.of(sfnt)` reads a font file's coverage from its tables — the `fvar` axes of a
      variable font, else the single weight and width the `OS/2` table records, the italic
      bits of `OS/2` and `post`, and the features of `GSUB` and `GPOS` — and `.combine` unions
      several files'. `Coverage(weights = …, italic = …)` declares the coverage of a source
      whose file is not to hand, a URL or an installed font; only what is declared is admitted,
      so a linked font declared at one weight rejects a request for another rather than
      letting the browser synthesize it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val file: Sfnt = Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter-BoldItalic.ttf")
      Coverage.of(file).complaint(Typeface["Inter"].bold.italic)    // Unset
      Coverage.of(file).complaint(Typeface["Inter"].light)          // UncoveredWeight("Inter", 300)

      val declared: Coverage = Coverage(weights = Weight.Light to Weight.Bold, italic = true)
      ```
  refer phoenicia.Typesettable
  refer phoenicia.Font
  refer phoenicia.Face
  entry .Entry   one face a provision offers, or the range a variable font offers
    detail
        The weights and widths spanned, whether the entry is upright, italic or both, the
        further axes with their ranges, and the layout features implemented — or `Unset` for
        the features of a face declared without a file, which then admits any feature.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val boldItalic = Coverage.Entry(Weight.Bold.range, false, true, Stretch.Normal.range, Nil, Nil)
        ```
    entry #weights   the span of weights
    entry #upright   whether an upright face is offered
    entry #italic   whether an italic face is offered
    entry #stretches   the span of widths
    entry #axes   the further variation axes, each with its range
    entry #features   the features implemented, or `Unset` if unknown
    entry #sets   whether the entry's weight, slant and stretch admit a face
    entry #complaint   the first variation or feature setting the entry cannot give, or `Unset`
        `complaint(name, variations, settings)` checks each variation's axis and value against
        `axes` (`UnknownAxis`, `AxisOutOfRange`) and each setting's feature against `features`
        (`MissingFeature`), naming the typeface in the reason. Unknown features admit anything.
  entry .apply   a declared coverage, for a source whose file is not to hand
      `Coverage(weights, upright, italic, stretches, axes)`, each with a default — regular,
      upright only, normal width, no axes — making one `Entry` with unknown features. For a
      URL or an installed font, whose faces are what the declaration says and nothing more.
  entry .of   a font file's coverage, read from its tables
      From a variable font's `fvar`: the `wght` axis as the weight span, `wdth` as the width
      span, an `ital` or `slnt` axis as offering both postures, and every axis as a
      `Variation.Range`. From a static font: the `OS/2` weight and width classes as single
      weights and widths. The italic bits of `OS/2` and a nonzero `post` italic angle make the entry
      italic. The features are `Sfnt#features`. A file whose tables cannot be read has
      `Unknown` coverage, which admits everything, rather than refusing faces it may well
      have.
  entry .combine   the union of several coverages; any `Unknown` makes the whole `Unknown`
  entry #complaint   why a face cannot be set from this coverage, or `Unset` if it can
      Two overloads. `complaint(face)` checks a whole face. `complaint(name, weight, slant,
      stretch, variations, features)` checks only what is given: a part passed as `Unset` is
      not checked, which is the form the compile-time check uses when a face's weight or slant
      is decided at runtime. `Unknown` coverage never complains.

entry .Font   a face paired with the provision for its typeface in a medium: what a renderer accepts
  detail
      A `Font in Web` is a face together with the `Typesettable` provision that makes its
      typeface available on the web; a `Font in Print` the same for a PDF. The medium is the
      font's `Form`, a type member it inherits from `Formal`: `Web`, `Print`, or whatever the
      font was made in. A renderer accepts fonts only in this form, so a document cannot name
      a typeface it does not carry a provision for; the document then collects the provisions
      of its fonts and emits whatever its medium needs.

      A font is made in one of two ways. `Font(face)` infers the medium from the expected
      type, which works for `Optional[Font in Web]` as well as `Font in Web`, so a field such
      as `Lettering(…, font = Font(inter.bold))` needs no annotation. `Font.of[Web](face)`
      names the medium, for where there is no expected type to infer it from. Both need a
      `Typeface of family is Typesettable in (? >: medium)` in scope — a provision for the
      typeface in that medium, or in every medium — and a `Tactic[Font.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      trait Paper extends Medium

      given inter: (Typeface of "Inter") is Typesettable in Medium =
        Typesettable.embedded(Sfnt(cp"/fonts/Inter.ttf"))

      val heading: Font in Paper = Font.of[Paper](Typeface["Inter"].bold.italic)
      val caption: Optional[Font in Paper] = Font(Typeface["Inter"].light)
      ```

      A face the provision cannot set — a weight it lacks, an italic it has not got, a
      feature its file does not implement, an axis value outside its range — is refused
      rather than synthesized or silently ignored. The refusal is a `Font.Error` when the font
      is made, naming the typeface and the problem. And where the provision's file is on the
      classpath (its `Locus`, from `Typesettable.embedded(cp"…")`) and the face's request is
      in its type, the refusal is a compile error with the same message, _phoenicia: the font
      at /fonts/Inter.ttf cannot set this face: Inter is not provided at weight 300_. Only
      what is known statically is checked statically; the runtime check always runs.

      A font shows as its face: `Inter 700 italic`.
  refer phoenicia.Face
  refer phoenicia.Typesettable
  refer phoenicia.Medium
  refer phoenicia.Coverage
  entry .Error   raised when a font cannot be read or a face cannot be set
    detail
        Error 564, _the font could not be used because …_, with a `Reason` whose number is the
        error's subcode. The table-reading reasons are raised by `Sfnt`'s accessors; the
        coverage reasons by `Font(…)`, `Font.of[…](…)` and `Font.paired` when the provision's
        `Coverage` refuses the face.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        def weightRefused(block: => Font in Medium): Boolean =
          try { block; false } catch
            case Font.Error(Font.Error.Reason.UncoveredWeight(_, _)) => true
            case _: Font.Error                                        => false
        ```
    entry .Reason   why: a missing or unreadable table, or a face the provision cannot set
      detail
          `MissingTable(tag)` (1), _the table `name` was not found_; `UnknownFormat(format)`
          (2), _the table contains data in unknown format n_, for a `cmap` subtable format
          the library does not read; `MagicNumber` (3), _the font did not contain expected
          check data_, for a `head` table with the wrong constant; `MissingEncoding` (4),
          _the font contains no usable character encoding_. Then the coverage reasons, each
          naming the typeface: `UncoveredWeight(typeface, weight)` (5), _Inter is not
          provided at weight 300_; `UncoveredSlant(typeface, slant)` (6), _Inter is not
          provided in an italic face_; `UncoveredStretch(typeface, stretch)` (7);
          `UnknownAxis(typeface, axis)` (8), _Inter has no variation axis opsz_;
          `AxisOutOfRange(typeface, axis, value)` (9); and `MissingFeature(typeface,
          feature)` (10), _Inter does not implement the layout feature onum_.
      entry #number   the reason's number, 1 to 10, which is the error's subcode
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as its message
    entry #reason   the reason
  entry .apply   `Font(face)`: pairs a face with the provision in scope, in the medium the expected type names
      An inline method whose medium is inferred from the expected type: `Font in Web`, or
      `Optional[Font in Web]`, since `Form` is a type member and the expected type pins it.
      Needs a `Typeface of family is Typesettable in (? >: medium)` and a `Tactic[Font.Error]`.
      Where the provision carries a `Locus` the face's recorded request is checked at compile
      time; the runtime check then runs regardless. Where there is no expected type, use `.of`.
  entry .of   `Font.of[Web](face)`: the same pairing with the medium named
      Returns an `.Of[medium]` whose `apply` takes the face, so that only the medium need be
      written and the family is inferred from the face. Cataclysm's `Web.font(face)` is
      `Font.of[Web](face)`.
  entry .Of   the helper `.of` returns, whose `apply` takes the face
    detail
        `Font.of[medium]` is an `Of[medium]`; `Font.of[medium](face)` is its `apply`. The two
        clauses are separate so the medium can be named without the family.
    entry #apply   pairs a face with the provision in scope for its typeface, in the named medium
  entry .paired   the runtime pairing the inline forms expand to: the coverage check, then the font
      `Font.paired[family, medium](face)` checks `provision.coverage.complaint(face)`, raises
      `Font.Error` with the reason if there is one, and otherwise makes the `Font in medium`.
      The inline `.apply` and `.of` add the compile-time check in front of it; code which
      needs no static check, or has a face whose request is not in its type, may call this
      directly.
  entry .showable   shows a font as its face: `Inter 700 italic`
  entry .inspectable   inspects a font as `Font(Face("Inter", Weight(700), Slant.Italic))`
  entry #face   the face
  entry #provision   the provision which makes the typeface available in the medium
  entry #typeface   the face's typeface

entry .internal   the opaque types `Glyph`, `Weight` and `Stretch`, and the macro behind `Font(…)`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `Glyph`, `Weight` and `Stretch` are declared here as opaque types,
      with their companions, and exported to the `phoenicia` package, where their pages are;
      the object exists so that the opaque types and the `Font` macro have a stable home.
  entry .Glyph   a glyph of one font; see `phoenicia.Glyph`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `Glyph`, exported with it to the `phoenicia` package.
    entry .apply   `Glyph(font, id)`: wraps a glyph id as a glyph of that font
  entry .Weight   a weight on the 1–1000 scale; see `phoenicia.Weight`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `Weight`, exported with it to the `phoenicia`
        package.
    entry .apply   a weight from an integer, clamped to 1–1000
    entry .Thin   weight 100
    entry .ExtraLight   weight 200
    entry .Light   weight 300
    entry .Regular   weight 400
    entry .Medium   weight 500
    entry .Semibold   weight 600
    entry .Bold   weight 700
    entry .ExtraBold   weight 800
    entry .Black   weight 900
    entry .Range   an inclusive span of weights
      entry #lower   the lightest weight in the span
      entry #upper   the heaviest weight in the span
      entry #contains   whether a weight lies within the span
    entry .showable   shows a weight as its number: `700`
    entry .inspectable   inspects a weight as `Weight(700)`
    entry .value   the weight as an integer
    entry .to   `lower to upper`: the inclusive range between two weights
    entry .range   the range of this weight alone
  entry .Stretch   a width as a percentage; see `phoenicia.Stretch`
    detail
        The companion of the opaque type `Stretch`, exported with it to the `phoenicia`
        package.
    entry .apply   a stretch from a percentage, clamped to 50–200
    entry .UltraCondensed   50%
    entry .ExtraCondensed   62.5%
    entry .Condensed   75%
    entry .SemiCondensed   87.5%
    entry .Normal   100%
    entry .SemiExpanded   112.5%
    entry .Expanded   125%
    entry .ExtraExpanded   150%
    entry .UltraExpanded   200%
    entry .widthClass   the stretch an OS/2 width class 1 to 9 names; `Normal` for 5 or an unknown value
    entry .Range   an inclusive span of stretches
      entry #lower   the narrowest stretch in the span
      entry #upper   the widest stretch in the span
      entry #contains   whether a stretch lies within the span
    entry .showable   shows a stretch as a percentage: `100.0%`
    entry .inspectable   inspects a stretch as `Stretch(100.0)`
    entry .percentage   the stretch as a percentage of normal width
    entry .to   `lower to upper`: the inclusive range between two stretches
    entry .range   the range of this stretch alone
  entry .id   the glyph's id within its font; 0 is the missing-glyph
  entry .font   the macro behind `Font(face)` and `Font.of[medium](face)`
      Reads the provision's `Locus` from its type; if it is a string literal naming a resource
      on the compiler's classpath, reads the font (once per path), derives its `Coverage` and
      checks the face's `Weights`, `Slanting` and `Enabled` type members against it, halting
      with _phoenicia: the font at `path` cannot set this face: …_ on refusal. Then expands to
      `Font.paired`.
